  'Human Evolution',
  'Human Evolution',
  'Human Evolution is a rigorous upper-division anthropology course on the origin, diversification, and continuing evolution of humans and our close relatives. The course integrates fossil morphology, archaeology, primate behavior, evolutionary theory, paleoecology, genetics, genomics, and ethical research practice. Students learn to evaluate evidence, compare competing models, and communicate human origins without linear-progress myths, biological race errors, or sensationalism.',
  $huevjson${"slug": "human-evolution", "title": "Human Evolution", "description": "Human Evolution is a rigorous upper-division anthropology course on the origin, diversification, and continuing evolution of humans and our close relatives. The course integrates fossil morphology, archaeology, primate behavior, evolutionary theory, paleoecology, genetics, genomics, and ethical research practice. Students learn to evaluate evidence, compare competing models, and communicate human origins without linear-progress myths, biological race errors, or sensationalism.", "topics": [{"id": "00b4d596-61c5-4a16-b225-ee6216bb80b4", "type": "topic", "title": "Human Evolution as Evidence-Based Inquiry", "description": "This opening unit frames human evolution as a scientific, interdisciplinary field rather than a simple origin story. Students learn how fossils, artifacts, living primates, ecology, and genomes become evidence.", "children": [{"id": "1d53783a-31a1-4f69-bf97-ac3c068d66e5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Questions, evidence, and inference", "children": [{"id": "c99f2619-b824-4e76-b993-9f851bcec67d", "type": "concept", "title": "Kinds of evidence: fossils, tools, sites, genomes, comparative anatomy"}, {"id": "909d3387-08fa-4f8d-bf35-391cd403a24c", "type": "concept", "title": "Hypotheses, alternative explanations, and uncertainty"}, {"id": "d3ab33ed-ce7b-4632-b169-7b059c5a591f", "type": "concept", "title": "Why human evolution is a branching history, not a ladder"}], "description": "The point is to understand how paleoanthropologists move from fragmentary evidence to defensible claims."}, {"id": "5a7dc8f1-cc3c-42de-a0b8-7543ed0e6014", "type": "subtopic", "title": "History of the field", "children": [{"id": "589ec79b-68bf-4dbc-9121-e3a3578aedc9", "type": "concept", "title": "Darwin, Huxley, early comparative anatomy"}, {"id": "0853f46c-e598-4c7e-b250-d6a471157cff", "type": "concept", "title": "Twentieth-century fossil discoveries and changing species concepts"}, {"id": "bc1b5d33-5b4a-46f9-af40-f4abb062c439", "type": "concept", "title": "Recent shifts from morphology-only models to integrated genomic evidence"}], "description": "Students see how the field changed as new methods, discoveries, and ethical standards challenged older assumptions."}, {"id": "e1d19974-29c0-4834-902e-00402a828793", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scientific literacy and public misconceptions", "children": [{"id": "0a7199fe-5011-4c5b-8487-bdeb30211951", "type": "concept", "title": "Evolution versus progress myths"}, {"id": "64133568-9161-4e2d-b1ac-aa1b7104f2b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Common ancestry versus direct descent"}, {"id": "ec5006da-67bf-4f1b-af32-6e8db956d183", "type": "concept", "title": "How to read popular claims about new fossils or ancient DNA"}], "description": "The gist is learning how to communicate human evolution clearly in public settings."}]}, {"id": "b4c7a19d-2aa3-4b78-a158-c8e36bee0631", "type": "topic", "title": "Evolutionary Theory, Phylogeny, and Deep Time", "description": "Human evolution rests on the same mechanisms that explain all life, but it demands comfort with long timescales and branching relationships. This unit gives students the conceptual grammar of the course.", "children": [{"id": "fa85c90d-d7f0-4a90-b968-04a5a0f2451e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Natural selection and evolutionary mechanisms", "children": [{"id": "f930f92c-f75c-4558-861e-b10f0a655ac7", "type": "concept", "title": "Selection, mutation, migration, recombination, and drift"}, {"id": "e7e51551-6254-4381-b6cc-2a39aaacb7ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Fitness, adaptation, constraint, and tradeoff"}, {"id": "7be9de90-af0d-4d5f-aa38-6a0f81334410", "type": "concept", "title": "Sexual selection and social behavior"}], "description": "Students learn the forces that change populations across generations."}, {"id": "6069dbdd-d14d-411c-900c-02573a5f5e01", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Phylogenetic thinking", "children": [{"id": "23de41c5-11fd-40f6-bb6d-325b99cc0659", "type": "concept", "title": "Clades, nodes, sister taxa, and outgroups"}, {"id": "d4c9b179-8123-4cb0-9385-8b30235d7aa5", "type": "concept", "title": "Homology, analogy, convergence, and mosaic evolution"}, {"id": "48fc5a8c-a4c7-4274-bd8c-194552e33940", "type": "concept", "title": "Parsimony, likelihood, and molecular clocks at a conceptual level"}], "description": "The point is to read evolutionary trees as hypotheses about relationship and descent."}, {"id": "44f6090a-f887-45f8-83a7-98baa4107562", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Geologic time and chronology", "children": [{"id": "34da1066-2d33-43c7-b889-7bdc7f1eb994", "type": "concept", "title": "Epochs relevant to human evolution: Miocene, Pliocene, Pleistocene, Holocene"}, {"id": "66447d9c-2142-4381-8746-bc14712c0087", "type": "concept", "title": "Relative and absolute dating logic"}, {"id": "b97b7c66-de52-40ec-8e36-5047f75eecea", "type": "concept", "title": "Uncertainty ranges and correlation across sites"}], "description": "Students practice anchoring fossils and events in deep time."}]}, {"id": "605cfcd5-5df7-441b-9782-559bb2b361f4", "type": "topic", "title": "Genetics, Genomics, and Population History", "description": "Modern human evolution cannot be world class without genomics. This unit connects inheritance, population genetics, ancient DNA, and ethical interpretation.", "children": [{"id": "badc3ed1-d119-4677-b658-fb29b2708b62", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Inheritance and population variation", "children": [{"id": "1a1a0092-dbda-4da3-b813-cb516686248e", "type": "concept", "title": "Alleles, genotype, phenotype, and environment"}, {"id": "588a3c9b-16db-45f1-a333-50b9ac4986b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Hardy-Weinberg reasoning and its limits"}, {"id": "1d023bc9-eb07-4d3a-a39b-424a5d521c66", "type": "concept", "title": "Population structure, admixture, and founder effects"}], "description": "The point is to connect individual inheritance to population-level evolutionary change."}, {"id": "3ba808f5-8e04-463f-b047-9c9ceacf2805", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Genomics and ancient DNA", "children": [{"id": "6f06a43b-1e26-42fc-a9bc-9e5d30b0023e", "type": "concept", "title": "Mitochondrial DNA, Y chromosomes, autosomal genomes"}, {"id": "b8e18819-5d51-4103-9289-7618d2f39928", "type": "concept", "title": "Ancient DNA preservation, contamination, and sampling bias"}, {"id": "3d9a2a69-3685-4ddd-9020-cd75ffb3aeb2", "type": "concept", "title": "Introgression from Neanderthals, Denisovans, and other archaic groups"}], "description": "Students learn how genomes revise fossil-only models and reveal contact among human groups."}, {"id": "7d4e3c07-8ed0-4273-a0de-a0c18352a9b7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Responsible genetic interpretation", "children": [{"id": "051738d1-a7c3-40a8-b68c-a41139b5bfe3", "type": "concept", "title": "Traits shaped by many genes and environments"}, {"id": "f11dc756-95ba-4200-a50e-435ac83f8ef4", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of ancestry inference"}, {"id": "be1bc47f-ceed-4f6e-8e56-17402bd0e6f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Consent, Indigenous data sovereignty, and community collaboration"}], "description": "The gist is to avoid genetic determinism while still taking genomic evidence seriously."}]}, {"id": "f2ef9819-67d0-4e13-9f6b-871c5c183592", "type": "topic", "title": "Primate Comparisons and the Last Common Ancestor", "description": "Living primates are not ancestors, but they are essential comparisons. This unit uses primate anatomy, behavior, ecology, and cognition to infer what may be ancient, derived, or convergent in humans.", "children": [{"id": "536e3a26-4206-4621-9082-11e42674bf63", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Primate taxonomy and adaptation", "children": [{"id": "6a1089e4-f6c8-4f67-8ddc-3e64a4336e3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Strepsirrhines, haplorhines, monkeys, apes, and hominins"}, {"id": "78ec49e7-91e9-4a2a-928f-037e25967a66", "type": "concept", "title": "Dental, locomotor, sensory, and reproductive traits"}, {"id": "d6b41f82-6b4b-4aca-a824-2bed72c844f5", "type": "concept", "title": "Ecological niches and life-history variation"}], "description": "Students learn where humans fit in primate diversity and why classification matters."}, {"id": "e94b25ca-d026-4261-a6e5-4b0d7916232b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ape behavior and social life", "children": [{"id": "21a15f26-a68f-4983-83a3-e605c1f9464e", "type": "concept", "title": "Chimpanzees, bonobos, gorillas, orangutans, and gibbons"}, {"id": "df7c85fd-9f84-4675-9d6d-49d91395cfd6", "type": "concept", "title": "Tool use, hunting, sharing, and cultural transmission"}, {"id": "bdd2d9da-1c65-4e2e-8a24-2098075af7c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Dominance, kinship, alliance, and care"}], "description": "The point is to compare patterns of cooperation, aggression, learning, and communication."}, {"id": "1445aaa2-4238-4087-8d7a-36f04817faad", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reconstructing the last common ancestor", "children": [{"id": "847eb934-bc9a-4e68-be4b-0082f3dba6f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Mosaic traits and missing fossil evidence"}, {"id": "005b1258-2939-4640-9ae2-7b033f5a9245", "type": "concept", "title": "Miocene ape diversity"}, {"id": "9fccd204-9778-436a-ac5e-905d0d8e67c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Locomotion, diet, and social behavior models"}], "description": "Students learn why the last common ancestor is a hypothesis, not a living chimpanzee."}]}, {"id": "edd20418-d267-4efc-920f-9254a9fe28d0", "type": "topic", "title": "Fossils, Taphonomy, Dating, and Paleoenvironmental Reconstruction", "description": "Fossils are data only after students understand how they form, survive, and get interpreted. This unit trains careful reading of sites, layers, dates, and environmental context.", "children": [{"id": "cd4a6ee7-4dad-40bb-a5de-1255fab0b8ed", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Taphonomy and fossilization", "children": [{"id": "d123bacd-f57e-479a-926a-a979748a99da", "type": "concept", "title": "Death assemblages, transport, burial, and preservation"}, {"id": "c7137f80-47b6-487c-bbec-f96ee773e309", "type": "concept", "title": "Bias by habitat, bone type, and discovery history"}, {"id": "fb662b73-7d6b-4ac8-aef8-e0ab6ac83eaa", "type": "concept", "title": "Distinguishing behavior from preservation pattern"}], "description": "The goal is to understand what the fossil record preserves and what it filters out."}, {"id": "c69d5936-2a59-4a1f-8384-749a644c85ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dating methods", "children": [{"id": "5f31a0b0-c512-4ee6-a75f-c9d57673fb11", "type": "concept", "title": "Stratigraphy, biostratigraphy, paleomagnetism"}, {"id": "763e953a-bbac-430e-9b3a-6188a4180484", "type": "concept", "title": "Radiometric approaches such as potassium-argon and argon-argon"}, {"id": "ec41297e-2051-4e8b-9c42-a14a9d865666", "type": "concept", "title": "Luminescence, electron spin resonance, and calibration problems"}], "description": "Students learn how ages are established and why multiple methods strengthen claims."}, {"id": "07ec3ccf-bf1b-42bb-ab8c-b817d7ddc8b2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Paleoenvironmental reconstruction", "children": [{"id": "cb05de40-cdfb-4f7f-b662-78f7f4cf48a6", "type": "concept", "title": "Stable isotopes, faunal communities, pollen, and sediments"}, {"id": "209f52cf-2c75-4db7-857a-c6e71f83c243", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate variability and habitat mosaics"}, {"id": "a16eb7e8-193d-407b-b53b-0ff62f832b05", "type": "concept", "title": "Site formation and landscape use"}], "description": "The point is to place hominins in changing landscapes rather than isolated specimens."}]}, {"id": "c573aa4f-44f7-44eb-9315-ec8680b88ef4", "type": "topic", "title": "Early Hominins and the Origins of Bipedalism", "description": "Bipedalism is one of the defining shifts in the hominin lineage. This unit asks why upright walking evolved and how early evidence is evaluated.", "children": [{"id": "72a61c86-d961-45e2-8bda-c085de240c81", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Candidate early hominins", "children": [{"id": "f132087f-6355-47c8-9f4a-50e2d9f61b05", "type": "concept", "title": "Sahelanthropus, Orrorin, Ardipithecus, and early Australopithecus"}, {"id": "f3173f5b-43de-4c83-9682-3e8234122e32", "type": "concept", "title": "Canine reduction, foramen magnum position, and postcranial hints"}, {"id": "b0b66c92-8b2e-4819-bef7-46bef5dbe479", "type": "concept", "title": "Species diagnosis with limited fossils"}], "description": "Students learn major fossils and why their status is debated."}, {"id": "9311abee-2136-4574-802b-e4b9f2698196", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Anatomy of bipedalism", "children": [{"id": "d979e2b4-174e-4f51-b671-4569541ec766", "type": "concept", "title": "Pelvis, femur, knee, foot arches, and spine"}, {"id": "76622a45-564c-4ad1-b061-e047d265c9bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Laetoli footprints and trackway evidence"}, {"id": "4cf8679b-b8a6-4bbc-8dac-690a5c90cff5", "type": "concept", "title": "Energetic, carrying, thermoregulatory, and ecological hypotheses"}], "description": "The gist is to connect bones to movement."}, {"id": "be904b89-9f82-4835-b771-d543e0fbc634", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mixed locomotion and ecology", "children": [{"id": "7a241a06-314b-4495-b8dc-e9105b69302d", "type": "concept", "title": "Arboreal traits and terrestrial adaptations"}, {"id": "657bffa8-82a4-45c6-892b-de92bd8cd3c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Woodland, savanna, and mosaic environments"}, {"id": "d4cbe8bf-8bfe-4ce8-b638-69de281233b2", "type": "concept", "title": "Tradeoffs in speed, endurance, and safety"}], "description": "Students see that early hominins may have combined walking with climbing."}]}, {"id": "0e88b791-e664-4383-8cbb-1d24e1b7ce51", "type": "topic", "title": "Australopiths and Adaptive Diversity", "description": "Australopiths show that early human relatives were diverse, regionally varied, and experimentally adapted. This unit replaces a single-line model with a branching adaptive radiation.", "children": [{"id": "462a1a7e-6282-4d43-8136-7784969f2235", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gracile australopiths", "children": [{"id": "a6561e40-dd01-453e-b26c-4b2e8d8f2f2d", "type": "concept", "title": "Australopithecus afarensis, africanus, anamensis, and sediba"}, {"id": "b44f9c1d-40cd-45e5-818a-5aadaa8b0101", "type": "concept", "title": "Cranial capacity, teeth, jaws, pelvis, hands, and feet"}, {"id": "40a3c10e-5d46-40ce-b980-35fc71ef0c3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Sexual dimorphism and social inference"}], "description": "Students learn the anatomy and significance of well-known species."}, {"id": "5716673a-d42d-4616-ab38-3d0fcc1f30b1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Robust australopiths and Paranthropus", "children": [{"id": "530b1885-8d5b-4eea-8765-f3fc0ab2ef60", "type": "concept", "title": "Massive jaws, sagittal crests, and megadontia"}, {"id": "004b93c4-bdcb-4d68-8e57-550d5b966786", "type": "concept", "title": "Dietary hypotheses and microwear/isotope evidence"}, {"id": "c399d85d-9996-4357-a2b8-7cad819f4fff", "type": "concept", "title": "Parallel lineages and adaptive dead ends"}], "description": "The point is to examine specialization and extinction."}, {"id": "99dbfc08-01b3-4cc3-ab34-2b97c500fdd0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Behavior, diet, and environment", "children": [{"id": "bc652ad9-f6a3-49e8-9d5e-71ab8f45fd61", "type": "concept", "title": "C3/C4 dietary signatures"}, {"id": "eebbf705-8b13-4875-8d8d-922f8c9a1c50", "type": "concept", "title": "Predation, scavenging, and landscape risk"}, {"id": "74ed4291-edd8-4144-aea9-418febd0c7d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Tool-use possibilities before genus Homo"}], "description": "Students connect morphology to foraging and habitat."}]}, {"id": "c6c2e79a-0002-44a7-89fc-fac38abb0739", "type": "topic", "title": "Origins of Genus Homo and Stone Tool Technologies", "description": "This unit investigates the emergence of larger brains, smaller teeth, new body plans, and durable technologies. Students learn why the origin of Homo remains contested.", "children": [{"id": "ec7c0054-01e7-49ff-87f7-7ad5bb939103", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Early Homo debates", "children": [{"id": "a94eef30-39b9-402c-9aa3-dd63f1a566e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Homo habilis, rudolfensis, and early erectus/ergaster"}, {"id": "7f7b3bb8-de61-4595-86e8-3fae31589a14", "type": "concept", "title": "Brain size, body size, hand anatomy, and teeth"}, {"id": "326cba55-5bdd-4a76-b630-c3a918c5f0da", "type": "concept", "title": "Variation, sexual dimorphism, and taxonomy disputes"}], "description": "The goal is to compare species definitions and competing models."}, {"id": "78b94bc0-34af-469b-af4b-3cceb63f54d2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Oldowan and early stone tools", "children": [{"id": "7e62ffcb-9c47-4a97-8993-070c38df39f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Cores, flakes, hammerstones, and reduction sequences"}, {"id": "03f717af-6cb8-4fe3-b94d-7d26ea0a9245", "type": "concept", "title": "Use-wear, cut marks, and butchery evidence"}, {"id": "d70d2902-a148-4cd8-8c43-e993ac0d6621", "type": "concept", "title": "Teaching, learning, and motor planning"}], "description": "Students learn how stone tools reveal cognition, skill, and site activity."}, {"id": "baac3b42-e733-40c1-83e9-8910b647639c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Diet, cooperation, and ecological flexibility", "children": [{"id": "d3b7ac5e-556e-4aa7-af5a-dc87056c0144", "type": "concept", "title": "Meat eating, marrow extraction, and plant foods"}, {"id": "935d090b-bc34-46ae-a8ce-49038fe47fd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Scavenging versus hunting debates"}, {"id": "1d0aeb84-3558-4679-9230-6046f29ebe47", "type": "concept", "title": "Food sharing, provisioning, and social organization"}], "description": "The gist is to connect technology to survival strategies."}]}, {"id": "562ae353-8999-4e63-b4bd-c9f8c5c24f90", "type": "topic", "title": "Homo erectus, Fire, Cooperation, and Global Dispersal", "description": "Homo erectus marks a major expansion in body form, technology, endurance, and geography. This unit studies how a flexible hominin became a long-lived global species complex.", "children": [{"id": "60b4c5f8-e074-4fde-92d0-7b837d000e9f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Anatomy and life history", "children": [{"id": "29ee46db-43cb-4a3e-ab0c-5c5646e4b3a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Tall stature, limb proportions, pelvis, and thermoregulation"}, {"id": "0bee341f-3213-42cc-85cb-a64d432d5018", "type": "concept", "title": "Brain expansion and dental reduction"}, {"id": "61f02aec-57a1-4a4c-b17e-3250c0a3c61d", "type": "concept", "title": "Growth, childhood, and aging evidence"}], "description": "Students examine the body plan that made long-distance movement possible."}, {"id": "1efc7315-427b-4768-baaa-92e6c4d7a48c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Acheulean technology and fire", "children": [{"id": "4fc535bf-c3ff-4af5-bd65-2ec8d051964c", "type": "concept", "title": "Handaxes, cleavers, and bifacial skill"}, {"id": "7cb4f628-7847-401c-be64-05fa2b2746e8", "type": "concept", "title": "Fire evidence, hearths, cooking, and warmth"}, {"id": "f231b86e-cb14-44a8-8b09-c6b68c9a666c", "type": "concept", "title": "Planning depth and raw material transport"}], "description": "The point is to understand technologies that reshaped behavior."}, {"id": "05a0071a-a074-471e-9fac-23fd61569e27", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Out-of-Africa dispersals", "children": [{"id": "673920f0-d448-4f08-a077-11936bfc0f69", "type": "concept", "title": "Dmanisi, East Asia, Southeast Asia, and island contexts"}, {"id": "62268b12-06b1-4769-acb5-955898ed3174", "type": "concept", "title": "Corridors, climate pulses, and ecological opportunity"}, {"id": "95a33d9d-d753-4f25-8b79-94cacc2a1eac", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional variation within erectus-like populations"}], "description": "Students learn how early Homo moved through varied environments."}]}, {"id": "32255e03-4989-4015-9ea0-798152afca33", "type": "topic", "title": "Middle Pleistocene Humans and the Road to Later Homo", "description": "The Middle Pleistocene is messy in the best scientific sense: many fossils, changing species names, and major divergence events. This unit trains students to handle uncertainty without oversimplifying.", "children": [{"id": "069867ac-8987-4b6d-a786-39ecc320ac2c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Species and taxonomy debates", "children": [{"id": "891359a1-eadc-4381-b0a7-c8c0220589fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional fossil variation"}, {"id": "94ded5c0-8121-4d73-8a59-61d5afb6f05a", "type": "concept", "title": "Brain size, face, brow ridges, and cranial shape"}, {"id": "aa663472-1427-4895-8e31-071bdaaf5dd2", "type": "concept", "title": "Chronospecies and lineage-splitting problems"}], "description": "The goal is to compare models of Homo heidelbergensis, rhodesiensis, antecessor, and related forms."}, {"id": "b25dcab4-43de-4e1e-ba11-d4141e5f9f41", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Behavioral and technological change", "children": [{"id": "1701496c-e2a6-4409-8cfd-19cd6d7a5cd9", "type": "concept", "title": "Prepared-core technologies and Levallois methods"}, {"id": "f5ad573c-01c3-4244-94de-c10777de6de9", "type": "concept", "title": "Big-game hunting and wooden spears"}, {"id": "72aff973-5304-49f6-92e2-cf6b6a22b4f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Shelters, landscapes, and social learning"}], "description": "Students connect fossils with archaeological traces."}, {"id": "b0da00f6-4a64-4f85-b36d-8fc9f58497c6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Divergence models", "children": [{"id": "7bb3d072-9cc4-456d-9c83-b11d8e9d0160", "type": "concept", "title": "African, European, and Asian population structure"}, {"id": "9f128380-e344-402e-8083-62e08c9d5f4c", "type": "concept", "title": "Genetic divergence estimates"}, {"id": "e43f25e1-c062-4283-9e32-eb40fe32736b", "type": "concept", "title": "Fossil evidence versus molecular evidence"}], "description": "The point is to link Middle Pleistocene fossils to Neanderthals, Denisovans, and sapiens."}]}, {"id": "9918fb51-30f5-48e0-a779-2dcbcaf60eee", "type": "topic", "title": "Neanderthals, Denisovans, Archaic Humans, and Ancient DNA", "description": "This unit shows how ancient DNA transformed human evolution from a replacement story into a history of contact, admixture, and regional adaptation.", "children": [{"id": "3fdd1be3-0283-49ac-a8b9-dbdc55d16b27", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Neanderthal biology and culture", "children": [{"id": "57467073-1ffb-44f8-b7c9-8f6b173e3354", "type": "concept", "title": "Cold-adapted anatomy and biomechanics"}, {"id": "5056a3c7-f7cc-468a-bef5-4580a92525ba", "type": "concept", "title": "Mousterian technology, hunting, care, and burial debates"}, {"id": "914e6c1b-34cb-4ca5-90a7-01457e3c1452", "type": "concept", "title": "Symbolism, pigments, ornaments, and cognition"}], "description": "Students learn Neanderthals as complex humans, not failed caricatures."}, {"id": "f8183f75-b66f-478a-b354-3fdde1290134", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Denisovans and other archaic groups", "children": [{"id": "fffc67fe-51b8-4185-98a7-2f9a6b0b2760", "type": "concept", "title": "Denisova Cave and Asian genomic signals"}, {"id": "32e33308-e791-4d2d-bd89-ef43c48bfc01", "type": "concept", "title": "Tibetan high-altitude adaptation and introgression"}, {"id": "98666e47-68b6-4938-8a1b-1b5b3cb67d57", "type": "concept", "title": "Ghost populations and limits of current evidence"}], "description": "The gist is to appreciate how DNA can identify populations with few fossils."}, {"id": "ef34c388-cfb2-4243-b1f6-7ba73cb07d86", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Admixture and replacement", "children": [{"id": "e561d93e-793a-4b01-8bab-fe45ec58846d", "type": "concept", "title": "Introgression percentages and regional differences"}, {"id": "41ac0481-eeeb-4025-b87b-cc1879f89e82", "type": "concept", "title": "Hybrid individuals and population contact zones"}, {"id": "421c4da0-1761-4793-b135-4c4a17f7cdf2", "type": "concept", "title": "Why extinction is not the same as genetic disappearance"}], "description": "Students compare models for interactions among Late Pleistocene populations."}]}, {"id": "433ebb6a-fa66-4d2f-bc30-c405180f0f5b", "type": "topic", "title": "Origins and Dispersals of Homo sapiens", "description": "This unit studies our species as an African lineage with complex dispersals, repeated contacts, and regional ecological adaptations. It avoids both triumphalist and simplistic migration narratives.", "children": [{"id": "9a6886a5-a0c2-4597-884d-2f911f5a9beb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "African origins", "children": [{"id": "87d09e3c-6786-4061-9db9-6668f9a58c1c", "type": "concept", "title": "Jebel Irhoud, Omo Kibish, Herto, and other African fossils"}, {"id": "8603ad6b-69ac-464b-b98f-7680384bb3d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Morphological modernity as a mosaic"}, {"id": "9302e2c5-5798-4408-b390-bdad140880c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Structured African populations and pan-African models"}], "description": "Students learn the fossil and genomic evidence for the emergence of Homo sapiens."}, {"id": "b9ab69dc-4c46-417a-9aa2-7a93cb34f282", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dispersal routes and timing", "children": [{"id": "b671a301-9f3b-49b0-95c3-03e7a01abe0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Levantine corridors, coastal routes, and inland pathways"}, {"id": "e06e0800-dcef-4886-8e83-1655eb6d62ec", "type": "concept", "title": "Australia, Eurasia, Sahul, and the Americas"}, {"id": "64c974c7-114c-438b-8590-f243da84eb5f", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate windows, sea level, and ecological opportunity"}], "description": "The point is to compare archaeological and genetic timelines."}, {"id": "5404f0cb-a945-421e-93ad-51f12a97dbfe", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modern human behavior", "children": [{"id": "1c6caaf4-8eda-498a-b823-1a945afaa00e", "type": "concept", "title": "Blades, bone tools, ornaments, pigment, and art"}, {"id": "b4b14f0e-1acc-4031-a95d-8c4d00deedcd", "type": "concept", "title": "Long-distance exchange and social networks"}, {"id": "730aa9fc-80bc-443b-9ac4-c7e75d3e0e5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Debates over gradual versus rapid behavioral change"}], "description": "Students evaluate evidence for symbolic, technological, and social changes."}]}, {"id": "6c8b1490-81a6-4a2b-8cdd-27530d1b5587", "type": "topic", "title": "Language, Symbolism, Art, and Social Learning", "description": "Human evolution is not only bones and genes; it is also the evolution of shared meaning. This unit investigates the biological and archaeological evidence for communication, teaching, and culture.", "children": [{"id": "f8ffc5dd-3d04-45b6-b477-840cb6eff03a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Language evolution", "children": [{"id": "d1c94f78-b87c-4a8e-bb70-9196b14d2d00", "type": "concept", "title": "Vocal tract, brain lateralization, FOXP2, and hearing anatomy"}, {"id": "e1fb71e2-bd3f-4204-99e3-d3e6382d7081", "type": "concept", "title": "Gesture, vocal learning, and multimodal communication"}, {"id": "f88d686f-231d-48d9-b950-af33a241b33f", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of direct inference from fossils"}], "description": "Students learn why language leaves indirect evidence and how scholars reason about it."}, {"id": "4d52f1d9-f616-4690-b34c-261cb5287ec5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Symbolism and art", "children": [{"id": "3b7ef025-6b48-45a0-9627-c2ab78d2430a", "type": "concept", "title": "Pigments, beads, engravings, figurines, and cave art"}, {"id": "66d5bc40-4e02-4528-a03f-77da33205722", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual, burial, and contested symbolic evidence"}, {"id": "0c75cc99-acd2-47f3-ad67-96ef30c823f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional traditions and social boundaries"}], "description": "The goal is to examine material signs of abstract thought and identity."}, {"id": "67e1377d-ab27-4b7a-9e0c-5aa16cd8f0b1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Cumulative culture and teaching", "children": [{"id": "0e635db9-3507-4a25-a620-358c4c873bd1", "type": "concept", "title": "Imitation, pedagogy, apprenticeship, and error correction"}, {"id": "a9cd6d6f-ff48-4cbc-a8d1-d10daf4b1831", "type": "concept", "title": "Social networks and innovation spread"}, {"id": "c58f1b57-22eb-410f-bfe8-618ee7efb2fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Niche construction and gene-culture coevolution"}], "description": "The point is to understand why human technology builds across generations."}]}, {"id": "4c21da56-20f3-4f28-9a99-80d247bb2feb", "type": "topic", "title": "Diet, Energetics, Life History, and Reproduction", "description": "Human bodies evolved around energy budgets, growth, reproduction, and care. This unit connects ecology and anatomy to the life course.", "children": [{"id": "168506e2-ca27-42e9-ae9f-1cc048fc016d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dietary reconstruction", "children": [{"id": "f0b47280-b160-4d69-8f57-412f74a5ff40", "type": "concept", "title": "Dental microwear, calculus, isotopes, residues, and tools"}, {"id": "f059aba4-764f-4b18-8ec3-4c47666c3132", "type": "concept", "title": "Meat, tubers, cooking, and plant processing"}, {"id": "c385dfb8-a8dd-4b71-a869-cdf30a10a201", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional and seasonal dietary flexibility"}], "description": "Students learn how scientists infer food from many evidence streams."}, {"id": "87209d83-9bcc-4e23-b505-af93463af31a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Energetics and brain evolution", "children": [{"id": "03eef3cc-fc3c-437b-be6d-2b40f986f953", "type": "concept", "title": "Expensive tissue hypothesis and alternatives"}, {"id": "aebc6505-8811-41ff-94d1-e5df006c3d09", "type": "concept", "title": "Cooking, gut size, endurance, and foraging returns"}, {"id": "a54ee573-de6b-4459-9c7c-780d418267bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Brain growth, childhood, and learning demands"}], "description": "The gist is to connect costly brains with food, cooperation, and development."}, {"id": "dc395717-0045-4c32-bb73-3dd8ec66f0c4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Life history and reproduction", "children": [{"id": "7e589342-957d-49e9-9cd3-b6a05917abae", "type": "concept", "title": "Birth constraints, pelvis, neonatal helplessness"}, {"id": "9afc73bb-c4ec-4ad2-aac1-be5397b1c778", "type": "concept", "title": "Alloparenting, grandparents, and kin support"}, {"id": "caca6068-26ef-4fba-b380-8f5376331147", "type": "concept", "title": "Mortality, fertility, and demographic inference"}], "description": "Students study the evolution of long childhoods, menopause, and cooperative care."}]}, {"id": "866a7940-c4e4-4601-ba44-541fc40008ac", "type": "topic", "title": "Human Variation, Adaptation, and the Critique of Race", "description": "This unit teaches human diversity with scientific precision and ethical care. Students learn why biological race is a poor explanation while adaptation, ancestry, and social racism remain essential topics.", "children": [{"id": "c7ae09a5-2479-4395-a946-e68951be2e73", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Patterns of human variation", "children": [{"id": "c34c6a66-b4e0-42e8-92cb-4ed2647031a1", "type": "concept", "title": "Skin color, body proportions, lactose persistence, altitude adaptation"}, {"id": "8f42b5f8-080d-4c3e-9d78-40daed16492b", "type": "concept", "title": "Standing variation, selection, drift, and migration"}, {"id": "0ce3ca27-e28c-48a7-a76b-500ea40a77f8", "type": "concept", "title": "Plasticity and developmental environment"}], "description": "The point is to understand clinal, local, and population-level variation."}, {"id": "97bdc882-1854-4d99-af39-7aecb28ffd8a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race, ancestry, and classification", "children": [{"id": "5f8c6341-ebcf-4807-9144-a025c74bff5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Within-group and between-group genetic variation"}, {"id": "cbacf936-d2c6-4929-aaba-a0d440c4cab7", "type": "concept", "title": "Clines versus discrete racial types"}, {"id": "4726c092-7fb5-4b5e-a29b-1422caab8581", "type": "concept", "title": "Forensic ancestry estimation and its limits"}], "description": "Students learn why race categories are social histories, not biological subspecies."}, {"id": "852aef6d-0b14-4b9e-8582-a09f518895c5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Misuse and responsible communication", "children": [{"id": "68492755-6b5f-45be-b419-472ecc8264c2", "type": "concept", "title": "Eugenics and typological anthropology"}, {"id": "652bdcd0-06e4-4c4e-a031-74184a73d5f5", "type": "concept", "title": "Genetic essentialism in media and politics"}, {"id": "c3baf078-7d83-400a-a5d9-bba6e62dbbc5", "type": "concept", "title": "Anti-racist biological anthropology practice"}], "description": "The gist is to confront scientific racism and communicate evidence clearly."}]}, {"id": "bd8cd96f-ad1b-4223-89c6-f1fe4d3eda32", "type": "topic", "title": "Evolutionary Medicine, Pathogens, and Contemporary Human Biology", "description": "Human evolution helps explain health without reducing people to genes. This unit connects past adaptations to present bodies, disease risks, and environmental change.", "children": [{"id": "8c2a36ec-482b-4272-9146-adc214fedfd8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mismatch and modern environments", "children": [{"id": "1515bb1d-ddf7-4824-89e7-8a2bc5a893d4", "type": "concept", "title": "Diet, activity, sleep, reproduction, and chronic disease"}, {"id": "d18826c6-8ecd-41df-93ae-241b8ce3a042", "type": "concept", "title": "Built environments and sedentary behavior"}, {"id": "8d03ee24-4575-4e2f-8acf-0eb068e74984", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits and critiques of mismatch explanations"}], "description": "Students learn how traits once useful can become harmful in new settings."}, {"id": "4ea3f536-182b-486c-b689-808fa0618657", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Pathogens and immunity", "children": [{"id": "6aa5bc05-a6ed-48e6-9a41-baaceff54c7d", "type": "concept", "title": "Co-evolution with microbes and parasites"}, {"id": "e6e67421-6cc4-4985-a10d-530bda1f700e", "type": "concept", "title": "Agriculture, settlement, zoonosis, and epidemics"}, {"id": "49290849-96c8-42bb-af31-f5abddba67a0", "type": "concept", "title": "HLA variation, sickle-cell trait, and selection examples"}], "description": "The goal is to see disease as an evolutionary relationship."}, {"id": "d90309a7-4650-474d-9331-f470f66b162f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Human evolution today", "children": [{"id": "5b4d69ff-46f5-4a8f-b5b4-0e976d37f7f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Recent selection signals"}, {"id": "34916b2a-059b-493c-9306-8baeaf44b739", "type": "concept", "title": "Urbanization, migration, and changing selection pressures"}, {"id": "0c5aa48e-d44d-442e-980e-bc9cba49deb5", "type": "concept", "title": "Cultural practices as evolutionary environments"}], "description": "The point is to recognize that evolution continues."}]}, {"id": "c97c1161-5538-4322-8922-929740efbf8e", "type": "topic", "title": "Ethics, Repatriation, Public Communication, and Misuse of Human Origins", "description": "World-class human evolution must be ethically literate. This unit addresses human remains, descendant communities, museums, genetics, and public debate.", "children": [{"id": "94448711-e9c4-406a-9f12-4280aa27c4e9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Human remains and repatriation", "children": [{"id": "0328c7a2-de00-4bfb-848c-eeac63494e7b", "type": "concept", "title": "NAGPRA and repatriation principles in the United States"}, {"id": "3e5cd418-3939-4313-a2ec-97e343791f83", "type": "concept", "title": "Museum collections, consent, and colonial collecting histories"}, {"id": "7fe754ca-8530-44c5-ac2e-22c7ba8b79ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Community-engaged research and stewardship"}], "description": "Students learn why bones are both evidence and ancestors."}, {"id": "8d881580-62d9-4b6a-bd86-095ffd99c8dc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ethics of fieldwork and genomics", "children": [{"id": "b9b1c8a0-f9f8-48ae-9867-0acc9538d94c", "type": "concept", "title": "Permits, local collaboration, and benefit sharing"}, {"id": "eedcbfb1-c1a4-436d-b860-295d354ecf4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Ancient DNA sampling decisions"}, {"id": "0bddd2e9-6e55-4c62-9b85-dda03ce8f7e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Data access, sovereignty, and interpretation rights"}], "description": "The gist is to build research practices that respect people, places, and data."}, {"id": "26ac9378-9b0e-4cd6-921d-e8cc9a9fb24c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public communication", "children": [{"id": "63b5d305-b36d-4e41-81c9-f36667aa01b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Teaching evolution with respect and clarity"}, {"id": "5a1ed6d4-38e3-447a-9e4d-cc4e06fc5bda", "type": "concept", "title": "Museum interpretation and digital media"}, {"id": "bcbc62c1-e581-45cb-b8ad-918e6a04320d", "type": "concept", "title": "Responding to sensational fossil and DNA headlines"}], "description": "Students practice explaining human origins in contested civic spaces."}]}, {"id": "9250f8f1-dba7-44e7-8eea-f40b78f1a6ae", "type": "topic", "title": "Capstone: Human Evolution Evidence Portfolio", "description": "The capstone asks students to synthesize evidence rather than memorize a timeline. Each student builds a portfolio around a debated question in human evolution and defends a careful interpretation.", "children": [{"id": "4b404de8-fc49-4ff3-8709-6e4336d91d72", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research question design", "children": [{"id": "bf9ca9a8-fa02-41fa-9331-412bb566af04", "type": "concept", "title": "Examples: bipedalism, sapiens dispersal, Neanderthal cognition, diet, adaptation"}, {"id": "e75e738e-0a84-480e-a18e-4d6a693e1ba0", "type": "concept", "title": "Scope, terms, and competing hypotheses"}, {"id": "5a16209e-5e3c-4ae2-965a-3d345a433664", "type": "concept", "title": "Source selection and evidence map"}], "description": "Students turn a broad topic into an answerable, evidence-based question."}, {"id": "323dfc36-bd62-4815-afa1-4fedfaa95fdc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Evidence portfolio", "children": [{"id": "206a1a9e-67f1-4a25-bc6a-47791c4fedc1", "type": "concept", "title": "Fossil or skeletal evidence"}, {"id": "a674a316-632e-4a75-806c-67bc6091993b", "type": "concept", "title": "Archaeological or environmental evidence"}, {"id": "79553040-7dfc-4514-9aea-24afa324233e", "type": "concept", "title": "Genetic, primate, or comparative evidence"}], "description": "The point is to combine multiple evidence types into a coherent argument."}, {"id": "20c1202e-ed89-48fb-87b7-9c543025d6a8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scientific communication", "children": [{"id": "1e8b0a17-c235-45be-8d22-2195ecb3abe3", "type": "concept", "title": "Annotated visual timeline or phylogeny"}, {"id": "2f233b7e-bcdc-4d62-adf7-c8a162ccf953", "type": "concept", "title": "Short research briefing and oral presentation"}, {"id": "8dcab090-a8ae-48de-8400-88317453090e", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflection on limits, ethics, and public misunderstanding"}], "description": "Students learn to present uncertainty, significance, and ethical stakes."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$huevjson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'human-evolution'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Human Evolution',
  goal = 'Human Evolution',
  summary = 'Human Evolution is a rigorous upper-division anthropology course on the origin, diversification, and continuing evolution of humans and our close relatives. The course integrates fossil morphology, archaeology, primate behavior, evolutionary theory, paleoecology, genetics, genomics, and ethical research practice. Students learn to evaluate evidence, compare competing models, and communicate human origins without linear-progress myths, biological race errors, or sensationalism.',
  data = $huevjson${"slug": "human-evolution", "title": "Human Evolution", "description": "Human Evolution is a rigorous upper-division anthropology course on the origin, diversification, and continuing evolution of humans and our close relatives. The course integrates fossil morphology, archaeology, primate behavior, evolutionary theory, paleoecology, genetics, genomics, and ethical research practice. Students learn to evaluate evidence, compare competing models, and communicate human origins without linear-progress myths, biological race errors, or sensationalism.", "topics": [{"id": "00b4d596-61c5-4a16-b225-ee6216bb80b4", "type": "topic", "title": "Human Evolution as Evidence-Based Inquiry", "description": "This opening unit frames human evolution as a scientific, interdisciplinary field rather than a simple origin story. Students learn how fossils, artifacts, living primates, ecology, and genomes become evidence.", "children": [{"id": "1d53783a-31a1-4f69-bf97-ac3c068d66e5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Questions, evidence, and inference", "children": [{"id": "c99f2619-b824-4e76-b993-9f851bcec67d", "type": "concept", "title": "Kinds of evidence: fossils, tools, sites, genomes, comparative anatomy"}, {"id": "909d3387-08fa-4f8d-bf35-391cd403a24c", "type": "concept", "title": "Hypotheses, alternative explanations, and uncertainty"}, {"id": "d3ab33ed-ce7b-4632-b169-7b059c5a591f", "type": "concept", "title": "Why human evolution is a branching history, not a ladder"}], "description": "The point is to understand how paleoanthropologists move from fragmentary evidence to defensible claims."}, {"id": "5a7dc8f1-cc3c-42de-a0b8-7543ed0e6014", "type": "subtopic", "title": "History of the field", "children": [{"id": "589ec79b-68bf-4dbc-9121-e3a3578aedc9", "type": "concept", "title": "Darwin, Huxley, early comparative anatomy"}, {"id": "0853f46c-e598-4c7e-b250-d6a471157cff", "type": "concept", "title": "Twentieth-century fossil discoveries and changing species concepts"}, {"id": "bc1b5d33-5b4a-46f9-af40-f4abb062c439", "type": "concept", "title": "Recent shifts from morphology-only models to integrated genomic evidence"}], "description": "Students see how the field changed as new methods, discoveries, and ethical standards challenged older assumptions."}, {"id": "e1d19974-29c0-4834-902e-00402a828793", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Scientific literacy and public misconceptions", "children": [{"id": "0a7199fe-5011-4c5b-8487-bdeb30211951", "type": "concept", "title": "Evolution versus progress myths"}, {"id": "64133568-9161-4e2d-b1ac-aa1b7104f2b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Common ancestry versus direct descent"}, {"id": "ec5006da-67bf-4f1b-af32-6e8db956d183", "type": "concept", "title": "How to read popular claims about new fossils or ancient DNA"}], "description": "The gist is learning how to communicate human evolution clearly in public settings."}]}, {"id": "b4c7a19d-2aa3-4b78-a158-c8e36bee0631", "type": "topic", "title": "Evolutionary Theory, Phylogeny, and Deep Time", "description": "Human evolution rests on the same mechanisms that explain all life, but it demands comfort with long timescales and branching relationships. This unit gives students the conceptual grammar of the course.", "children": [{"id": "fa85c90d-d7f0-4a90-b968-04a5a0f2451e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Natural selection and evolutionary mechanisms", "children": [{"id": "f930f92c-f75c-4558-861e-b10f0a655ac7", "type": "concept", "title": "Selection, mutation, migration, recombination, and drift"}, {"id": "e7e51551-6254-4381-b6cc-2a39aaacb7ff", "type": "concept", "title": "Fitness, adaptation, constraint, and tradeoff"}, {"id": "7be9de90-af0d-4d5f-aa38-6a0f81334410", "type": "concept", "title": "Sexual selection and social behavior"}], "description": "Students learn the forces that change populations across generations."}, {"id": "6069dbdd-d14d-411c-900c-02573a5f5e01", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Phylogenetic thinking", "children": [{"id": "23de41c5-11fd-40f6-bb6d-325b99cc0659", "type": "concept", "title": "Clades, nodes, sister taxa, and outgroups"}, {"id": "d4c9b179-8123-4cb0-9385-8b30235d7aa5", "type": "concept", "title": "Homology, analogy, convergence, and mosaic evolution"}, {"id": "48fc5a8c-a4c7-4274-bd8c-194552e33940", "type": "concept", "title": "Parsimony, likelihood, and molecular clocks at a conceptual level"}], "description": "The point is to read evolutionary trees as hypotheses about relationship and descent."}, {"id": "44f6090a-f887-45f8-83a7-98baa4107562", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Geologic time and chronology", "children": [{"id": "34da1066-2d33-43c7-b889-7bdc7f1eb994", "type": "concept", "title": "Epochs relevant to human evolution: Miocene, Pliocene, Pleistocene, Holocene"}, {"id": "66447d9c-2142-4381-8746-bc14712c0087", "type": "concept", "title": "Relative and absolute dating logic"}, {"id": "b97b7c66-de52-40ec-8e36-5047f75eecea", "type": "concept", "title": "Uncertainty ranges and correlation across sites"}], "description": "Students practice anchoring fossils and events in deep time."}]}, {"id": "605cfcd5-5df7-441b-9782-559bb2b361f4", "type": "topic", "title": "Genetics, Genomics, and Population History", "description": "Modern human evolution cannot be world class without genomics. This unit connects inheritance, population genetics, ancient DNA, and ethical interpretation.", "children": [{"id": "badc3ed1-d119-4677-b658-fb29b2708b62", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Inheritance and population variation", "children": [{"id": "1a1a0092-dbda-4da3-b813-cb516686248e", "type": "concept", "title": "Alleles, genotype, phenotype, and environment"}, {"id": "588a3c9b-16db-45f1-a333-50b9ac4986b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Hardy-Weinberg reasoning and its limits"}, {"id": "1d023bc9-eb07-4d3a-a39b-424a5d521c66", "type": "concept", "title": "Population structure, admixture, and founder effects"}], "description": "The point is to connect individual inheritance to population-level evolutionary change."}, {"id": "3ba808f5-8e04-463f-b047-9c9ceacf2805", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Genomics and ancient DNA", "children": [{"id": "6f06a43b-1e26-42fc-a9bc-9e5d30b0023e", "type": "concept", "title": "Mitochondrial DNA, Y chromosomes, autosomal genomes"}, {"id": "b8e18819-5d51-4103-9289-7618d2f39928", "type": "concept", "title": "Ancient DNA preservation, contamination, and sampling bias"}, {"id": "3d9a2a69-3685-4ddd-9020-cd75ffb3aeb2", "type": "concept", "title": "Introgression from Neanderthals, Denisovans, and other archaic groups"}], "description": "Students learn how genomes revise fossil-only models and reveal contact among human groups."}, {"id": "7d4e3c07-8ed0-4273-a0de-a0c18352a9b7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Responsible genetic interpretation", "children": [{"id": "051738d1-a7c3-40a8-b68c-a41139b5bfe3", "type": "concept", "title": "Traits shaped by many genes and environments"}, {"id": "f11dc756-95ba-4200-a50e-435ac83f8ef4", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of ancestry inference"}, {"id": "be1bc47f-ceed-4f6e-8e56-17402bd0e6f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Consent, Indigenous data sovereignty, and community collaboration"}], "description": "The gist is to avoid genetic determinism while still taking genomic evidence seriously."}]}, {"id": "f2ef9819-67d0-4e13-9f6b-871c5c183592", "type": "topic", "title": "Primate Comparisons and the Last Common Ancestor", "description": "Living primates are not ancestors, but they are essential comparisons. This unit uses primate anatomy, behavior, ecology, and cognition to infer what may be ancient, derived, or convergent in humans.", "children": [{"id": "536e3a26-4206-4621-9082-11e42674bf63", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Primate taxonomy and adaptation", "children": [{"id": "6a1089e4-f6c8-4f67-8ddc-3e64a4336e3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Strepsirrhines, haplorhines, monkeys, apes, and hominins"}, {"id": "78ec49e7-91e9-4a2a-928f-037e25967a66", "type": "concept", "title": "Dental, locomotor, sensory, and reproductive traits"}, {"id": "d6b41f82-6b4b-4aca-a824-2bed72c844f5", "type": "concept", "title": "Ecological niches and life-history variation"}], "description": "Students learn where humans fit in primate diversity and why classification matters."}, {"id": "e94b25ca-d026-4261-a6e5-4b0d7916232b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ape behavior and social life", "children": [{"id": "21a15f26-a68f-4983-83a3-e605c1f9464e", "type": "concept", "title": "Chimpanzees, bonobos, gorillas, orangutans, and gibbons"}, {"id": "df7c85fd-9f84-4675-9d6d-49d91395cfd6", "type": "concept", "title": "Tool use, hunting, sharing, and cultural transmission"}, {"id": "bdd2d9da-1c65-4e2e-8a24-2098075af7c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Dominance, kinship, alliance, and care"}], "description": "The point is to compare patterns of cooperation, aggression, learning, and communication."}, {"id": "1445aaa2-4238-4087-8d7a-36f04817faad", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reconstructing the last common ancestor", "children": [{"id": "847eb934-bc9a-4e68-be4b-0082f3dba6f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Mosaic traits and missing fossil evidence"}, {"id": "005b1258-2939-4640-9ae2-7b033f5a9245", "type": "concept", "title": "Miocene ape diversity"}, {"id": "9fccd204-9778-436a-ac5e-905d0d8e67c7", "type": "concept", "title": "Locomotion, diet, and social behavior models"}], "description": "Students learn why the last common ancestor is a hypothesis, not a living chimpanzee."}]}, {"id": "edd20418-d267-4efc-920f-9254a9fe28d0", "type": "topic", "title": "Fossils, Taphonomy, Dating, and Paleoenvironmental Reconstruction", "description": "Fossils are data only after students understand how they form, survive, and get interpreted. This unit trains careful reading of sites, layers, dates, and environmental context.", "children": [{"id": "cd4a6ee7-4dad-40bb-a5de-1255fab0b8ed", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Taphonomy and fossilization", "children": [{"id": "d123bacd-f57e-479a-926a-a979748a99da", "type": "concept", "title": "Death assemblages, transport, burial, and preservation"}, {"id": "c7137f80-47b6-487c-bbec-f96ee773e309", "type": "concept", "title": "Bias by habitat, bone type, and discovery history"}, {"id": "fb662b73-7d6b-4ac8-aef8-e0ab6ac83eaa", "type": "concept", "title": "Distinguishing behavior from preservation pattern"}], "description": "The goal is to understand what the fossil record preserves and what it filters out."}, {"id": "c69d5936-2a59-4a1f-8384-749a644c85ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dating methods", "children": [{"id": "5f31a0b0-c512-4ee6-a75f-c9d57673fb11", "type": "concept", "title": "Stratigraphy, biostratigraphy, paleomagnetism"}, {"id": "763e953a-bbac-430e-9b3a-6188a4180484", "type": "concept", "title": "Radiometric approaches such as potassium-argon and argon-argon"}, {"id": "ec41297e-2051-4e8b-9c42-a14a9d865666", "type": "concept", "title": "Luminescence, electron spin resonance, and calibration problems"}], "description": "Students learn how ages are established and why multiple methods strengthen claims."}, {"id": "07ec3ccf-bf1b-42bb-ab8c-b817d7ddc8b2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Paleoenvironmental reconstruction", "children": [{"id": "cb05de40-cdfb-4f7f-b662-78f7f4cf48a6", "type": "concept", "title": "Stable isotopes, faunal communities, pollen, and sediments"}, {"id": "209f52cf-2c75-4db7-857a-c6e71f83c243", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate variability and habitat mosaics"}, {"id": "a16eb7e8-193d-407b-b53b-0ff62f832b05", "type": "concept", "title": "Site formation and landscape use"}], "description": "The point is to place hominins in changing landscapes rather than isolated specimens."}]}, {"id": "c573aa4f-44f7-44eb-9315-ec8680b88ef4", "type": "topic", "title": "Early Hominins and the Origins of Bipedalism", "description": "Bipedalism is one of the defining shifts in the hominin lineage. This unit asks why upright walking evolved and how early evidence is evaluated.", "children": [{"id": "72a61c86-d961-45e2-8bda-c085de240c81", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Candidate early hominins", "children": [{"id": "f132087f-6355-47c8-9f4a-50e2d9f61b05", "type": "concept", "title": "Sahelanthropus, Orrorin, Ardipithecus, and early Australopithecus"}, {"id": "f3173f5b-43de-4c83-9682-3e8234122e32", "type": "concept", "title": "Canine reduction, foramen magnum position, and postcranial hints"}, {"id": "b0b66c92-8b2e-4819-bef7-46bef5dbe479", "type": "concept", "title": "Species diagnosis with limited fossils"}], "description": "Students learn major fossils and why their status is debated."}, {"id": "9311abee-2136-4574-802b-e4b9f2698196", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Anatomy of bipedalism", "children": [{"id": "d979e2b4-174e-4f51-b671-4569541ec766", "type": "concept", "title": "Pelvis, femur, knee, foot arches, and spine"}, {"id": "76622a45-564c-4ad1-b061-e047d265c9bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Laetoli footprints and trackway evidence"}, {"id": "4cf8679b-b8a6-4bbc-8dac-690a5c90cff5", "type": "concept", "title": "Energetic, carrying, thermoregulatory, and ecological hypotheses"}], "description": "The gist is to connect bones to movement."}, {"id": "be904b89-9f82-4835-b771-d543e0fbc634", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mixed locomotion and ecology", "children": [{"id": "7a241a06-314b-4495-b8dc-e9105b69302d", "type": "concept", "title": "Arboreal traits and terrestrial adaptations"}, {"id": "657bffa8-82a4-45c6-892b-de92bd8cd3c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Woodland, savanna, and mosaic environments"}, {"id": "d4cbe8bf-8bfe-4ce8-b638-69de281233b2", "type": "concept", "title": "Tradeoffs in speed, endurance, and safety"}], "description": "Students see that early hominins may have combined walking with climbing."}]}, {"id": "0e88b791-e664-4383-8cbb-1d24e1b7ce51", "type": "topic", "title": "Australopiths and Adaptive Diversity", "description": "Australopiths show that early human relatives were diverse, regionally varied, and experimentally adapted. This unit replaces a single-line model with a branching adaptive radiation.", "children": [{"id": "462a1a7e-6282-4d43-8136-7784969f2235", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Gracile australopiths", "children": [{"id": "a6561e40-dd01-453e-b26c-4b2e8d8f2f2d", "type": "concept", "title": "Australopithecus afarensis, africanus, anamensis, and sediba"}, {"id": "b44f9c1d-40cd-45e5-818a-5aadaa8b0101", "type": "concept", "title": "Cranial capacity, teeth, jaws, pelvis, hands, and feet"}, {"id": "40a3c10e-5d46-40ce-b980-35fc71ef0c3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Sexual dimorphism and social inference"}], "description": "Students learn the anatomy and significance of well-known species."}, {"id": "5716673a-d42d-4616-ab38-3d0fcc1f30b1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Robust australopiths and Paranthropus", "children": [{"id": "530b1885-8d5b-4eea-8765-f3fc0ab2ef60", "type": "concept", "title": "Massive jaws, sagittal crests, and megadontia"}, {"id": "004b93c4-bdcb-4d68-8e57-550d5b966786", "type": "concept", "title": "Dietary hypotheses and microwear/isotope evidence"}, {"id": "c399d85d-9996-4357-a2b8-7cad819f4fff", "type": "concept", "title": "Parallel lineages and adaptive dead ends"}], "description": "The point is to examine specialization and extinction."}, {"id": "99dbfc08-01b3-4cc3-ab34-2b97c500fdd0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Behavior, diet, and environment", "children": [{"id": "bc652ad9-f6a3-49e8-9d5e-71ab8f45fd61", "type": "concept", "title": "C3/C4 dietary signatures"}, {"id": "eebbf705-8b13-4875-8d8d-922f8c9a1c50", "type": "concept", "title": "Predation, scavenging, and landscape risk"}, {"id": "74ed4291-edd8-4144-aea9-418febd0c7d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Tool-use possibilities before genus Homo"}], "description": "Students connect morphology to foraging and habitat."}]}, {"id": "c6c2e79a-0002-44a7-89fc-fac38abb0739", "type": "topic", "title": "Origins of Genus Homo and Stone Tool Technologies", "description": "This unit investigates the emergence of larger brains, smaller teeth, new body plans, and durable technologies. Students learn why the origin of Homo remains contested.", "children": [{"id": "ec7c0054-01e7-49ff-87f7-7ad5bb939103", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Early Homo debates", "children": [{"id": "a94eef30-39b9-402c-9aa3-dd63f1a566e9", "type": "concept", "title": "Homo habilis, rudolfensis, and early erectus/ergaster"}, {"id": "7f7b3bb8-de61-4595-86e8-3fae31589a14", "type": "concept", "title": "Brain size, body size, hand anatomy, and teeth"}, {"id": "326cba55-5bdd-4a76-b630-c3a918c5f0da", "type": "concept", "title": "Variation, sexual dimorphism, and taxonomy disputes"}], "description": "The goal is to compare species definitions and competing models."}, {"id": "78b94bc0-34af-469b-af4b-3cceb63f54d2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Oldowan and early stone tools", "children": [{"id": "7e62ffcb-9c47-4a97-8993-070c38df39f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Cores, flakes, hammerstones, and reduction sequences"}, {"id": "03f717af-6cb8-4fe3-b94d-7d26ea0a9245", "type": "concept", "title": "Use-wear, cut marks, and butchery evidence"}, {"id": "d70d2902-a148-4cd8-8c43-e993ac0d6621", "type": "concept", "title": "Teaching, learning, and motor planning"}], "description": "Students learn how stone tools reveal cognition, skill, and site activity."}, {"id": "baac3b42-e733-40c1-83e9-8910b647639c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Diet, cooperation, and ecological flexibility", "children": [{"id": "d3b7ac5e-556e-4aa7-af5a-dc87056c0144", "type": "concept", "title": "Meat eating, marrow extraction, and plant foods"}, {"id": "935d090b-bc34-46ae-a8ce-49038fe47fd5", "type": "concept", "title": "Scavenging versus hunting debates"}, {"id": "1d0aeb84-3558-4679-9230-6046f29ebe47", "type": "concept", "title": "Food sharing, provisioning, and social organization"}], "description": "The gist is to connect technology to survival strategies."}]}, {"id": "562ae353-8999-4e63-b4bd-c9f8c5c24f90", "type": "topic", "title": "Homo erectus, Fire, Cooperation, and Global Dispersal", "description": "Homo erectus marks a major expansion in body form, technology, endurance, and geography. This unit studies how a flexible hominin became a long-lived global species complex.", "children": [{"id": "60b4c5f8-e074-4fde-92d0-7b837d000e9f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Anatomy and life history", "children": [{"id": "29ee46db-43cb-4a3e-ab0c-5c5646e4b3a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Tall stature, limb proportions, pelvis, and thermoregulation"}, {"id": "0bee341f-3213-42cc-85cb-a64d432d5018", "type": "concept", "title": "Brain expansion and dental reduction"}, {"id": "61f02aec-57a1-4a4c-b17e-3250c0a3c61d", "type": "concept", "title": "Growth, childhood, and aging evidence"}], "description": "Students examine the body plan that made long-distance movement possible."}, {"id": "1efc7315-427b-4768-baaa-92e6c4d7a48c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Acheulean technology and fire", "children": [{"id": "4fc535bf-c3ff-4af5-bd65-2ec8d051964c", "type": "concept", "title": "Handaxes, cleavers, and bifacial skill"}, {"id": "7cb4f628-7847-401c-be64-05fa2b2746e8", "type": "concept", "title": "Fire evidence, hearths, cooking, and warmth"}, {"id": "f231b86e-cb14-44a8-8b09-c6b68c9a666c", "type": "concept", "title": "Planning depth and raw material transport"}], "description": "The point is to understand technologies that reshaped behavior."}, {"id": "05a0071a-a074-471e-9fac-23fd61569e27", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Out-of-Africa dispersals", "children": [{"id": "673920f0-d448-4f08-a077-11936bfc0f69", "type": "concept", "title": "Dmanisi, East Asia, Southeast Asia, and island contexts"}, {"id": "62268b12-06b1-4769-acb5-955898ed3174", "type": "concept", "title": "Corridors, climate pulses, and ecological opportunity"}, {"id": "95a33d9d-d753-4f25-8b79-94cacc2a1eac", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional variation within erectus-like populations"}], "description": "Students learn how early Homo moved through varied environments."}]}, {"id": "32255e03-4989-4015-9ea0-798152afca33", "type": "topic", "title": "Middle Pleistocene Humans and the Road to Later Homo", "description": "The Middle Pleistocene is messy in the best scientific sense: many fossils, changing species names, and major divergence events. This unit trains students to handle uncertainty without oversimplifying.", "children": [{"id": "069867ac-8987-4b6d-a786-39ecc320ac2c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Species and taxonomy debates", "children": [{"id": "891359a1-eadc-4381-b0a7-c8c0220589fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional fossil variation"}, {"id": "94ded5c0-8121-4d73-8a59-61d5afb6f05a", "type": "concept", "title": "Brain size, face, brow ridges, and cranial shape"}, {"id": "aa663472-1427-4895-8e31-071bdaaf5dd2", "type": "concept", "title": "Chronospecies and lineage-splitting problems"}], "description": "The goal is to compare models of Homo heidelbergensis, rhodesiensis, antecessor, and related forms."}, {"id": "b25dcab4-43de-4e1e-ba11-d4141e5f9f41", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Behavioral and technological change", "children": [{"id": "1701496c-e2a6-4409-8cfd-19cd6d7a5cd9", "type": "concept", "title": "Prepared-core technologies and Levallois methods"}, {"id": "f5ad573c-01c3-4244-94de-c10777de6de9", "type": "concept", "title": "Big-game hunting and wooden spears"}, {"id": "72aff973-5304-49f6-92e2-cf6b6a22b4f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Shelters, landscapes, and social learning"}], "description": "Students connect fossils with archaeological traces."}, {"id": "b0da00f6-4a64-4f85-b36d-8fc9f58497c6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Divergence models", "children": [{"id": "7bb3d072-9cc4-456d-9c83-b11d8e9d0160", "type": "concept", "title": "African, European, and Asian population structure"}, {"id": "9f128380-e344-402e-8083-62e08c9d5f4c", "type": "concept", "title": "Genetic divergence estimates"}, {"id": "e43f25e1-c062-4283-9e32-eb40fe32736b", "type": "concept", "title": "Fossil evidence versus molecular evidence"}], "description": "The point is to link Middle Pleistocene fossils to Neanderthals, Denisovans, and sapiens."}]}, {"id": "9918fb51-30f5-48e0-a779-2dcbcaf60eee", "type": "topic", "title": "Neanderthals, Denisovans, Archaic Humans, and Ancient DNA", "description": "This unit shows how ancient DNA transformed human evolution from a replacement story into a history of contact, admixture, and regional adaptation.", "children": [{"id": "3fdd1be3-0283-49ac-a8b9-dbdc55d16b27", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Neanderthal biology and culture", "children": [{"id": "57467073-1ffb-44f8-b7c9-8f6b173e3354", "type": "concept", "title": "Cold-adapted anatomy and biomechanics"}, {"id": "5056a3c7-f7cc-468a-bef5-4580a92525ba", "type": "concept", "title": "Mousterian technology, hunting, care, and burial debates"}, {"id": "914e6c1b-34cb-4ca5-90a7-01457e3c1452", "type": "concept", "title": "Symbolism, pigments, ornaments, and cognition"}], "description": "Students learn Neanderthals as complex humans, not failed caricatures."}, {"id": "f8183f75-b66f-478a-b354-3fdde1290134", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Denisovans and other archaic groups", "children": [{"id": "fffc67fe-51b8-4185-98a7-2f9a6b0b2760", "type": "concept", "title": "Denisova Cave and Asian genomic signals"}, {"id": "32e33308-e791-4d2d-bd89-ef43c48bfc01", "type": "concept", "title": "Tibetan high-altitude adaptation and introgression"}, {"id": "98666e47-68b6-4938-8a1b-1b5b3cb67d57", "type": "concept", "title": "Ghost populations and limits of current evidence"}], "description": "The gist is to appreciate how DNA can identify populations with few fossils."}, {"id": "ef34c388-cfb2-4243-b1f6-7ba73cb07d86", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Admixture and replacement", "children": [{"id": "e561d93e-793a-4b01-8bab-fe45ec58846d", "type": "concept", "title": "Introgression percentages and regional differences"}, {"id": "41ac0481-eeeb-4025-b87b-cc1879f89e82", "type": "concept", "title": "Hybrid individuals and population contact zones"}, {"id": "421c4da0-1761-4793-b135-4c4a17f7cdf2", "type": "concept", "title": "Why extinction is not the same as genetic disappearance"}], "description": "Students compare models for interactions among Late Pleistocene populations."}]}, {"id": "433ebb6a-fa66-4d2f-bc30-c405180f0f5b", "type": "topic", "title": "Origins and Dispersals of Homo sapiens", "description": "This unit studies our species as an African lineage with complex dispersals, repeated contacts, and regional ecological adaptations. It avoids both triumphalist and simplistic migration narratives.", "children": [{"id": "9a6886a5-a0c2-4597-884d-2f911f5a9beb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "African origins", "children": [{"id": "87d09e3c-6786-4061-9db9-6668f9a58c1c", "type": "concept", "title": "Jebel Irhoud, Omo Kibish, Herto, and other African fossils"}, {"id": "8603ad6b-69ac-464b-b98f-7680384bb3d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Morphological modernity as a mosaic"}, {"id": "9302e2c5-5798-4408-b390-bdad140880c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Structured African populations and pan-African models"}], "description": "Students learn the fossil and genomic evidence for the emergence of Homo sapiens."}, {"id": "b9ab69dc-4c46-417a-9aa2-7a93cb34f282", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dispersal routes and timing", "children": [{"id": "b671a301-9f3b-49b0-95c3-03e7a01abe0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Levantine corridors, coastal routes, and inland pathways"}, {"id": "e06e0800-dcef-4886-8e83-1655eb6d62ec", "type": "concept", "title": "Australia, Eurasia, Sahul, and the Americas"}, {"id": "64c974c7-114c-438b-8590-f243da84eb5f", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate windows, sea level, and ecological opportunity"}], "description": "The point is to compare archaeological and genetic timelines."}, {"id": "5404f0cb-a945-421e-93ad-51f12a97dbfe", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Modern human behavior", "children": [{"id": "1c6caaf4-8eda-498a-b823-1a945afaa00e", "type": "concept", "title": "Blades, bone tools, ornaments, pigment, and art"}, {"id": "b4b14f0e-1acc-4031-a95d-8c4d00deedcd", "type": "concept", "title": "Long-distance exchange and social networks"}, {"id": "730aa9fc-80bc-443b-9ac4-c7e75d3e0e5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Debates over gradual versus rapid behavioral change"}], "description": "Students evaluate evidence for symbolic, technological, and social changes."}]}, {"id": "6c8b1490-81a6-4a2b-8cdd-27530d1b5587", "type": "topic", "title": "Language, Symbolism, Art, and Social Learning", "description": "Human evolution is not only bones and genes; it is also the evolution of shared meaning. This unit investigates the biological and archaeological evidence for communication, teaching, and culture.", "children": [{"id": "f8ffc5dd-3d04-45b6-b477-840cb6eff03a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Language evolution", "children": [{"id": "d1c94f78-b87c-4a8e-bb70-9196b14d2d00", "type": "concept", "title": "Vocal tract, brain lateralization, FOXP2, and hearing anatomy"}, {"id": "e1fb71e2-bd3f-4204-99e3-d3e6382d7081", "type": "concept", "title": "Gesture, vocal learning, and multimodal communication"}, {"id": "f88d686f-231d-48d9-b950-af33a241b33f", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of direct inference from fossils"}], "description": "Students learn why language leaves indirect evidence and how scholars reason about it."}, {"id": "4d52f1d9-f616-4690-b34c-261cb5287ec5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Symbolism and art", "children": [{"id": "3b7ef025-6b48-45a0-9627-c2ab78d2430a", "type": "concept", "title": "Pigments, beads, engravings, figurines, and cave art"}, {"id": "66d5bc40-4e02-4528-a03f-77da33205722", "type": "concept", "title": "Ritual, burial, and contested symbolic evidence"}, {"id": "0c75cc99-acd2-47f3-ad67-96ef30c823f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional traditions and social boundaries"}], "description": "The goal is to examine material signs of abstract thought and identity."}, {"id": "67e1377d-ab27-4b7a-9e0c-5aa16cd8f0b1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Cumulative culture and teaching", "children": [{"id": "0e635db9-3507-4a25-a620-358c4c873bd1", "type": "concept", "title": "Imitation, pedagogy, apprenticeship, and error correction"}, {"id": "a9cd6d6f-ff48-4cbc-a8d1-d10daf4b1831", "type": "concept", "title": "Social networks and innovation spread"}, {"id": "c58f1b57-22eb-410f-bfe8-618ee7efb2fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Niche construction and gene-culture coevolution"}], "description": "The point is to understand why human technology builds across generations."}]}, {"id": "4c21da56-20f3-4f28-9a99-80d247bb2feb", "type": "topic", "title": "Diet, Energetics, Life History, and Reproduction", "description": "Human bodies evolved around energy budgets, growth, reproduction, and care. This unit connects ecology and anatomy to the life course.", "children": [{"id": "168506e2-ca27-42e9-ae9f-1cc048fc016d", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Dietary reconstruction", "children": [{"id": "f0b47280-b160-4d69-8f57-412f74a5ff40", "type": "concept", "title": "Dental microwear, calculus, isotopes, residues, and tools"}, {"id": "f059aba4-764f-4b18-8ec3-4c47666c3132", "type": "concept", "title": "Meat, tubers, cooking, and plant processing"}, {"id": "c385dfb8-a8dd-4b71-a869-cdf30a10a201", "type": "concept", "title": "Regional and seasonal dietary flexibility"}], "description": "Students learn how scientists infer food from many evidence streams."}, {"id": "87209d83-9bcc-4e23-b505-af93463af31a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Energetics and brain evolution", "children": [{"id": "03eef3cc-fc3c-437b-be6d-2b40f986f953", "type": "concept", "title": "Expensive tissue hypothesis and alternatives"}, {"id": "aebc6505-8811-41ff-94d1-e5df006c3d09", "type": "concept", "title": "Cooking, gut size, endurance, and foraging returns"}, {"id": "a54ee573-de6b-4459-9c7c-780d418267bd", "type": "concept", "title": "Brain growth, childhood, and learning demands"}], "description": "The gist is to connect costly brains with food, cooperation, and development."}, {"id": "dc395717-0045-4c32-bb73-3dd8ec66f0c4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Life history and reproduction", "children": [{"id": "7e589342-957d-49e9-9cd3-b6a05917abae", "type": "concept", "title": "Birth constraints, pelvis, neonatal helplessness"}, {"id": "9afc73bb-c4ec-4ad2-aac1-be5397b1c778", "type": "concept", "title": "Alloparenting, grandparents, and kin support"}, {"id": "caca6068-26ef-4fba-b380-8f5376331147", "type": "concept", "title": "Mortality, fertility, and demographic inference"}], "description": "Students study the evolution of long childhoods, menopause, and cooperative care."}]}, {"id": "866a7940-c4e4-4601-ba44-541fc40008ac", "type": "topic", "title": "Human Variation, Adaptation, and the Critique of Race", "description": "This unit teaches human diversity with scientific precision and ethical care. Students learn why biological race is a poor explanation while adaptation, ancestry, and social racism remain essential topics.", "children": [{"id": "c7ae09a5-2479-4395-a946-e68951be2e73", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Patterns of human variation", "children": [{"id": "c34c6a66-b4e0-42e8-92cb-4ed2647031a1", "type": "concept", "title": "Skin color, body proportions, lactose persistence, altitude adaptation"}, {"id": "8f42b5f8-080d-4c3e-9d78-40daed16492b", "type": "concept", "title": "Standing variation, selection, drift, and migration"}, {"id": "0ce3ca27-e28c-48a7-a76b-500ea40a77f8", "type": "concept", "title": "Plasticity and developmental environment"}], "description": "The point is to understand clinal, local, and population-level variation."}, {"id": "97bdc882-1854-4d99-af39-7aecb28ffd8a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race, ancestry, and classification", "children": [{"id": "5f8c6341-ebcf-4807-9144-a025c74bff5a", "type": "concept", "title": "Within-group and between-group genetic variation"}, {"id": "cbacf936-d2c6-4929-aaba-a0d440c4cab7", "type": "concept", "title": "Clines versus discrete racial types"}, {"id": "4726c092-7fb5-4b5e-a29b-1422caab8581", "type": "concept", "title": "Forensic ancestry estimation and its limits"}], "description": "Students learn why race categories are social histories, not biological subspecies."}, {"id": "852aef6d-0b14-4b9e-8582-a09f518895c5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Misuse and responsible communication", "children": [{"id": "68492755-6b5f-45be-b419-472ecc8264c2", "type": "concept", "title": "Eugenics and typological anthropology"}, {"id": "652bdcd0-06e4-4c4e-a031-74184a73d5f5", "type": "concept", "title": "Genetic essentialism in media and politics"}, {"id": "c3baf078-7d83-400a-a5d9-bba6e62dbbc5", "type": "concept", "title": "Anti-racist biological anthropology practice"}], "description": "The gist is to confront scientific racism and communicate evidence clearly."}]}, {"id": "bd8cd96f-ad1b-4223-89c6-f1fe4d3eda32", "type": "topic", "title": "Evolutionary Medicine, Pathogens, and Contemporary Human Biology", "description": "Human evolution helps explain health without reducing people to genes. This unit connects past adaptations to present bodies, disease risks, and environmental change.", "children": [{"id": "8c2a36ec-482b-4272-9146-adc214fedfd8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mismatch and modern environments", "children": [{"id": "1515bb1d-ddf7-4824-89e7-8a2bc5a893d4", "type": "concept", "title": "Diet, activity, sleep, reproduction, and chronic disease"}, {"id": "d18826c6-8ecd-41df-93ae-241b8ce3a042", "type": "concept", "title": "Built environments and sedentary behavior"}, {"id": "8d03ee24-4575-4e2f-8acf-0eb068e74984", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits and critiques of mismatch explanations"}], "description": "Students learn how traits once useful can become harmful in new settings."}, {"id": "4ea3f536-182b-486c-b689-808fa0618657", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Pathogens and immunity", "children": [{"id": "6aa5bc05-a6ed-48e6-9a41-baaceff54c7d", "type": "concept", "title": "Co-evolution with microbes and parasites"}, {"id": "e6e67421-6cc4-4985-a10d-530bda1f700e", "type": "concept", "title": "Agriculture, settlement, zoonosis, and epidemics"}, {"id": "49290849-96c8-42bb-af31-f5abddba67a0", "type": 